Subject: [PATCH] d3d10umd: preload the native d3d12.dll in a Chromium GPU process

The package puts its Direct3D 12 bridge (vkd3d-proton) in place of
System32's d3d12.dll. A Chromium GPU process (Chrome, Edge, Electron,
WebView2, CEF) takes only Microsoft-signed DLLs once it is running
(MicrosoftSignedOnly). When a page makes it look for GPU video encoders it
delay-loads d3d12.dll, Windows refuses the bridge (error 577), and the
process ends itself on purpose (media::CreateD3D12Device ->
HandleDelayLoadFailureCommon, exception 0x80000003). After three such
crashes the browser runs without a GPU: no WebGL (GitHub #238).

Such a process opens this driver's adapter before that restriction starts.
OpenAdapterCommon now loads Microsoft's d3d12.dll there, from the copy the
installer keeps as System32\vmlab-win3d\native\d3d12.dll (SysWOW64 for a
32-bit process), when the command line has --type=gpu-process and no
d3d12.dll is loaded yet. The loader resolves the later load of the bare
name "d3d12.dll" to the module of that name that is already in the process,
so the copy must keep the file name. The GPU process then has what stock
Windows gives it; every other process still gets the bridge.

D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_NATIVE_D3D12_PRELOAD=0 in yttrium.ini turns it off.

Evidence: docs/chromium.md, docs/evidence/chromium/.
Origin: GitHub VMLabDev/vmlab#238 (card #140), carried by card #147.
---
diff --git a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
index e850214..ee9bae3 100644
--- a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,9 @@
 
 #include "util/u_memory.h"
 
+#include <string.h>
+#include <wchar.h>
+
 
 static HRESULT APIENTRY CloseAdapter(D3D10DDI_HADAPTER hAdapter);
 
@@ -58,6 +61,55 @@ ExposeD3D11_1()
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * The driver package puts its Direct3D 12 bridge (vkd3d-proton) in place of
+ * System32's d3d12.dll.  The bridge is not signed by Microsoft, and a
+ * Chromium GPU process (Chrome, Edge, Electron, WebView2, CEF) takes only
+ * Microsoft-signed DLLs once it has started: its later delay-load of
+ * d3d12.dll is refused (error 577) and it ends itself on purpose.
+ *
+ * Such a process opens this driver's adapter first, before that restriction.
+ * So load Microsoft's own d3d12.dll here, from the copy the package keeps
+ * under the same file name in another folder: the loader resolves a later
+ * load of the bare name "d3d12.dll" to a module of that name that is already
+ * loaded, whatever its folder.  The process then has what stock Windows gives
+ * it (no Direct3D 12 device on this adapter), and every other process the
+ * bridge.
+ */
+static void
+PreloadNativeD3D12ForChromiumGpuProcess(void)
+{
+   static LONG done;
+   if (InterlockedExchange(&done, 1))
+      return;
+
+   if (!yttrium_gdi_debug_get_bool_option(
+          "D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_NATIVE_D3D12_PRELOAD", true))
+      return;
+
+   const wchar_t *command_line = GetCommandLineW();
+   if (!command_line || !wcsstr(command_line, L"--type=gpu-process"))
+      return;
+
+   if (GetModuleHandleW(L"d3d12.dll"))
+      return;
+
+   static const wchar_t tail[] = L"\\vmlab-win3d\\native\\d3d12.dll";
+   wchar_t path[MAX_PATH];
+   const UINT len = GetSystemDirectoryW(path, MAX_PATH);
+   if (!len || len + sizeof(tail) / sizeof(tail[0]) > MAX_PATH)
+      return;
+   memcpy(path + len, tail, sizeof(tail));
+
+   if (GetFileAttributesW(path) == INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES)
+      return;
+
+   HMODULE native = LoadLibraryExW(path, NULL, LOAD_WITH_ALTERED_SEARCH_PATH);
+   yttrium_gdi_user_logf("d3d10umd: Chromium GPU process, native d3d12.dll %s error=%lu\n",
+                         native ? "preloaded" : "not loaded",
+                         native ? 0ul : (unsigned long)GetLastError());
+}
+
 /*
  * ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  *
@@ -79,6 +131,8 @@ OpenAdapterCommon(__inout D3D10DDIARG_OPENADAPTER *pOpenData)   // IN
 #endif
    ++numAdapters;
 
+   PreloadNativeD3D12ForChromiumGpuProcess();
+
    Adapter *pAdaptor = (Adapter *)calloc(sizeof *pAdaptor, 1);
    if (!pAdaptor) {
       --numAdapters;
